Two issues:
1. `Property 'seed' is used before its initialization` — a field initializer `private readonly characterGround = new CharacterGround(this.seed);` runs before the constructor body assigns `this.seed`? No — `this.seed` is a `private readonly seed: string` declared as `private readonly seed: string;` and assigned in the constructor. Field initializers run before the constructor body in declaration order. Since the `seed` field is declared (around line 294) before `characterGround` (line 398), the initializer `new CharacterGround(this.seed)` runs at a point when `this.seed` is still undefined (seed is assigned inside the constructor body after the field initializers).

Fix: declare `characterGround` without an initializer, and assign it inside the constructor after `this.seed` is set… or, more simply, make it lazy. The cleanest way: move the field declaration to have no initializer, and assign it in the constructor. In fact, `this.seed = seed` is set at the very start of the constructor body (line 423: `this.seed = seed;`). Field initializers run before the constructor body. So do it like this:

```ts
private characterGround: CharacterGround;
...
constructor(...) {
  this.seed = seed;
  this.characterGround = new CharacterGround(seed);
  ...
}
```

